\section{Conjectural Automorphic Classification of Build Systems}

\begin{conjecture}[Automorphic Orbit of Self-Referential Build Systems]
Let $\mathcal{B}$ denote the category of self-referential build systems, whose
objects are triples $(G, R, \Phi)$ consisting of a dependency graph $G$, a set
of rewrite or build rules $R$, and an evaluation or execution map
$\Phi : (G,R) \to (G,R)$ that resolves dependencies and produces a fixed point
artifact in the same configuration space.  Let morphisms in $\mathcal{B}$ be
structure-preserving transformations of such triples.

Define the automorphism group
\\\[
\mathrm{Aut}(\mathcal{C}) = \mathrm{Aut}(\text{configuration manifold})
\\\]
acting on $\mathcal{B}$ by precomposition on the rule space $R$ and by graph
automorphisms on $G$.

We conjecture that the classical build systems {\tt Make}, {\tt Autotools},
and {\tt Cargo} belong to a single \emph{automorphic orbit} under the action
of $\mathrm{Aut}(\mathcal{C})$.  More precisely, their associated triples
\\\[
$(G_{\text{make}}, R_{\text{make}}, \Phi_{\text{make}}),\qquad
(G_{\text{auto}}, R_{\text{auto}}, \Phi_{\text{auto}}),\qquad
(G_{\text{cargo}}, R_{\text{cargo}}, \Phi_{\text{cargo}})$
\\\]
are conjecturally situated in the same orbit of $\mathcal{B}$ as elements of an
automorphic representation of $\mathrm{Aut}(\mathcal{C})$.

Equivalently, there exists a sequence of structure-preserving Hecke-like
correspondences transforming any one of these build systems into another
without leaving the orbit type determined by the triple $(G,R,\Phi)$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{remark}
This conjecture places these build systems into a unified automorphic
framework, analogous to the classification of modular forms, paramodular
surfaces, and higher-rank automorphic representations within the LMFDB.  The
shared orbit type arises from their common property of being self-referential,
closure-seeking, and structure-preserving transformations of their own
configuration manifolds.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
The analogy suggests that traditional build systems can be interpreted as
computational analogues of objects in the Langlands program: they exhibit
recursive descent, fixed-point behavior, internal symmetries, and
self-consistency constraints reminiscent of automorphic forms under Hecke
operators.
\end{remark}